/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/cysa-plus.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("cysa-plus", [["cy1",0,1,"An analyst sees a workstation sending hundreds of DNS queries per minute to a single external domain. Each query has a long, random-looking subdomain. What is the most likely explanation?",["DNS cache poisoning","DNS tunneling used for command and control or exfiltration","Domain generation algorithm lookups against many different domains","Normal content delivery network traffic"],1,"Encoding data in long subdomains of one domain is a classic DNS tunneling pattern. A DGA produces many different domains rather than many subdomains of one.","Objective 1.2",["DNS cache poisoning corrupts a resolver's cached answers so users are sent to a malicious IP; it does not generate a flood of outbound queries with long random subdomains.",null,"A DGA makes malware query many different generated domain names, not many random subdomains under one single domain.","CDN traffic uses a modest number of predictable hostnames and would not produce hundreds of queries a minute with long random labels to one domain."],2],["cy2",0,1,"NetFlow shows a host connecting to the same external IP every 60 seconds, around the clock, with nearly identical packet sizes. What does this most likely indicate?",["Beaconing to a command-and-control server","A port scan","ARP spoofing","Scheduled backup traffic"],0,"Regular intervals and uniform sizes to one destination are the signature of malware beaconing.","Objective 1.2",[null,"A port scan touches many ports or hosts in a short burst; it does not repeat a connection to one IP at a fixed interval around the clock.","ARP spoofing is a local-segment attack that forges MAC-to-IP mappings and would not appear as regular connections to an external IP in NetFlow.","Backups usually run on a schedule such as nightly and move large, varying volumes of data, not tiny identical packets every 60 seconds all day."],1],["cy3",0,1,"An analyst needs to know which process on a Windows endpoint opened a suspicious outbound connection. Which log source is best?",["Perimeter firewall logs","DHCP logs","Sysmon network connection events (Event ID 3)","NetFlow records"],2,"Sysmon Event ID 3 records the process image that made each network connection. Firewall and NetFlow data show IPs and ports but not the process.","Objective 1.3",["Perimeter firewall logs show source and destination IPs and ports, but they cannot see which process on the endpoint opened the connection.","DHCP logs map IP addresses to MAC addresses and hostnames over time; they record nothing about processes or outbound connections.",null,"NetFlow summarizes conversations by IP, port and byte count at the network level and has no visibility into the process that created them."],2],["cy4",0,1,"Windows Security logs show 300 Event ID 4625 entries for one account from a single external IP, followed by an Event ID 4624 for the same account and IP. What most likely happened?",["The account lockout policy worked as designed","Privilege escalation through a service account","The attacker cleared the audit log","A password-guessing attack succeeded"],3,"4625 is a failed logon and 4624 a successful one; many failures then a success from the same source indicates a successful brute-force or guessing attack.","Objective 1.2",["If the lockout policy had worked, the account would have locked after a few failures and there would be no successful 4624 logon from the attacker's IP.","Nothing here shows an account gaining higher rights; the pattern is repeated failed logons followed by a success for the same account, not privilege escalation.","Clearing the audit log generates Event ID 1102, and the failed and successful logon events are still present, so the log was not cleared.",null],3],["cy5",0,1,"Tier 1 analysts spend most of each shift manually looking up sender reputation, URLs and attachment hashes for reported phishing emails. What is the best way to improve efficiency?",["Add more SIEM correlation rules","Hire more tier 1 analysts","Use a SOAR playbook to automate the enrichment steps","Increase log retention"],2,"Repetitive, well-defined enrichment is exactly what SOAR automation and orchestration are for.","Objective 1.5",["More correlation rules would create more alerts, but they would not remove the manual lookup work analysts do on each reported phishing email.","Hiring adds cost and headcount while leaving the same slow, repetitive manual process in place; it does not improve efficiency.",null,"Longer retention keeps data around longer but does nothing to speed up the reputation and hash lookups analysts perform by hand."],2],["cy6",0,1,"A threat hunting team wants detections that will be hardest for an adversary to evade by changing their tools. What should they focus on?",["File hashes","IP addresses","Domain names","Tactics, techniques and procedures"],3,"On the Pyramid of Pain, TTPs are the hardest indicators for an attacker to change; hashes and IPs are trivial to change.","Objective 1.4",["File hashes sit at the bottom of the Pyramid of Pain; changing a single byte of the malware produces a new hash and evades the detection.","Attackers can switch IP addresses cheaply and quickly, so IP-based detections are easy to evade.","Domains are only slightly harder to change than IPs; attackers routinely register new ones, so they are not the hardest indicator to evade.",null],3],["cy7",0,1,"Two organizations want to automatically exchange threat intelligence between their platforms over HTTPS. Which standard defines the transport protocol for this?",["STIX","TAXII","OpenIOC","YARA"],1,"TAXII is the transport protocol for sharing threat intelligence; STIX is the data format being carried.","Objective 1.4",["STIX is the structured language and data format for describing threat intelligence, not the transport protocol that moves it between platforms.",null,"OpenIOC is an XML format for describing indicators of compromise; it does not define an HTTPS transport for exchanging them.","YARA is a rule language for matching patterns in files and memory, not a protocol for sharing intelligence between organizations."],1],["cy8",0,1,"After reading a report that attackers in its sector are abusing built-in admin tools, a SOC decides to search its environment proactively. What should the team do first?",["Develop a hypothesis based on the threat intelligence","Wait for the SIEM to generate alerts","Reimage all administrator workstations","Run a full vulnerability scan"],0,"Threat hunting starts with a hypothesis that guides which data to collect and search.","Objective 1.4",[null,"Waiting for SIEM alerts is reactive detection; threat hunting is proactive searching for activity that existing alerts may be missing.","Reimaging all admin workstations is a disruptive remediation step taken without any evidence of compromise; hunting starts with investigation, not cleanup.","A vulnerability scan finds missing patches and misconfigurations, but it will not reveal attackers abusing legitimate built-in admin tools."],2],["cy9",0,1,"EDR shows winword.exe spawning powershell.exe with the -EncodedCommand parameter shortly after a user opened an invoice attachment. What is the most likely cause?",["A normal Office update process","A malicious macro launching encoded PowerShell","A memory leak in Word","The user running a scheduled task"],1,"Office apps should not spawn PowerShell; an encoded command right after opening an attachment points to a malicious macro.","Objective 1.2",["Office updates run through the Click-to-Run update service, not by having winword.exe launch PowerShell with an encoded command.",null,"A memory leak causes growing memory use or crashes; it does not make Word spawn a new PowerShell process with encoded arguments.","A scheduled task would be launched by the Task Scheduler service, not as a child of winword.exe right after an attachment was opened."],2],["cy10",0,1,"An analyst finds a string in a web log that appears to be Base64-encoded, gzip-compressed data. Which free tool is best suited to decode it quickly?",["Nmap","Hashcat","CyberChef","Metasploit"],2,"CyberChef chains decoding operations such as From Base64 and Gunzip in one recipe.","Objective 1.3",["Nmap is a network scanner for discovering hosts, ports and services; it has no features for decoding Base64 or decompressing gzip data.","Hashcat cracks password hashes; Base64 and gzip are reversible encodings and compression, not hashes, so there is nothing to crack.",null,"Metasploit is an exploitation framework for penetration testing, not a tool for decoding and decompressing strings found in logs."],1],["cy11",0,1,"Which email authentication mechanism lets a domain owner publish a policy telling receivers to quarantine or reject messages that fail alignment checks?",["SPF","DKIM","DMARC","S/MIME"],2,"DMARC builds on SPF and DKIM, checks alignment with the From domain and publishes a none/quarantine/reject policy.","Objective 1.3",["SPF lists which IPs may send mail for a domain, but it has no mechanism to publish a quarantine or reject policy or to check alignment with the From header.","DKIM adds a cryptographic signature to messages, but it does not tell receivers what to do when checks fail.",null,"S/MIME encrypts and signs individual messages end to end using certificates; it is not a DNS-published domain policy for receiving servers."],1],["cy12",0,1,"An analyst wants to see what a suspicious attachment does when executed, including files dropped and domains contacted, without risking production systems. What is the best approach?",["Open it on the analyst workstation with antivirus disabled","Submit only its hash to a reputation service","Detonate it in an isolated sandbox","Email it to the vendor for review"],2,"A sandbox safely observes runtime behavior. A hash lookup only helps if the file is already known.","Objective 1.3",["Running unknown malware on an analyst workstation with antivirus disabled risks infecting the analyst's machine and spreading into the production network.","A hash lookup only tells you something if the exact file is already known; it shows no runtime behavior such as dropped files or contacted domains.",null,"Emailing malware to a vendor is slow, may be blocked in transit and does not give the analyst the behavioral details needed now."],1],["cy13",0,1,"While building a timeline in the SIEM, an analyst notices a firewall event appears to happen before the phishing email that caused it. What is the most likely cause?",["Log retention is too short","The SIEM license limit was reached","Verbose logging is enabled","The systems are not synchronized to a common time source"],3,"Without NTP synchronization, timestamps drift and correlated events appear out of order.","Objective 1.1",["Short retention would cause older events to be missing entirely, not appear with timestamps in the wrong order.","Hitting a SIEM license limit can drop or delay ingestion, but it does not change the timestamps recorded by the source devices.","Verbose logging increases the amount of detail recorded but does not affect whether the source clocks agree with each other.",null],3],["cy14",0,1,"A company with a mostly remote workforce wants to combine SD-WAN with cloud-delivered secure web gateway, CASB and zero trust network access. Which architecture fits?",["Secure access service edge (SASE)","VLAN segmentation","Network access control","A jump box"],0,"SASE converges networking (SD-WAN) and cloud-delivered security services such as SWG, CASB and ZTNA.","Objective 1.1",[null,"VLAN segmentation separates traffic on local switches; it does not deliver cloud-based security services to remote users or combine them with SD-WAN.","NAC checks devices before they join the local network, which does little for a mostly remote workforce and is not a cloud-delivered security stack.","A jump box is a single hardened host for administrative access, not an architecture that combines SD-WAN with SWG, CASB and ZTNA."],1],["cy15",0,1,"Auditors find that 40 administrators hold permanent domain admin rights. Which control best reduces this standing privilege?",["A shared administrator account","Privileged access management with just-in-time elevation","Longer password requirements","Single sign-on"],1,"PAM with just-in-time access grants elevated rights only when needed and for a limited time.","Objective 1.1",["A shared admin account removes individual accountability and still leaves standing privilege; it makes the problem worse, not better.",null,"Longer passwords make credentials harder to guess, but the 40 accounts would still hold permanent domain admin rights.","SSO makes sign-in more convenient across applications but does not reduce the amount or duration of privileged access."],2],["cy16",0,1,"At 3 a.m., a finance file server uploads 40 GB to a consumer cloud storage service it has never contacted before. Which indicator does this best match?",["Data exfiltration","Denial of service","Rogue device","Beaconing"],0,"A large, unusual outbound transfer to a new destination at an odd hour is a data exfiltration indicator.","Objective 1.2",[null,"Denial of service overwhelms a target to make it unavailable; a single server uploading data to cloud storage is outbound transfer, not an availability attack.","A rogue device is unauthorized hardware on the network; this is a known finance server behaving abnormally, not a new device.","Beaconing is small, regular check-ins to a C2 server; a single 40 GB upload is a bulk transfer, which points to exfiltration."],2],["cy17",0,1,"A switch reports a new MAC address with an unknown vendor OUI on a conference room port. Which control would best prevent unauthorized devices from getting network access this way?",["A stronger Wi-Fi passphrase","A host-based intrusion detection system","Data loss prevention","802.1X port-based network access control"],3,"802.1X/NAC requires devices to authenticate before a wired port grants access.","Objective 1.1",["A Wi-Fi passphrase protects wireless access, but this device is plugged into a wired switch port in the conference room.","A HIDS runs on individual hosts and cannot stop an unknown device from being granted access through a switch port.","DLP watches for sensitive data leaving the organization; it does not control which devices can connect to the network.",null],3],["cy18",0,1,"A web server log contains the request: /products.php?id=5' OR '1'='1. Which attack is being attempted?",["Cross-site scripting","Directory traversal","SQL injection","Command injection"],2,"The quote and always-true OR condition are aimed at altering a SQL query.","Objective 1.2",["XSS injects script such as a script tag into pages viewed by other users; this payload uses quotes and an always-true OR condition aimed at a database query.","Directory traversal uses ../ sequences to reach files outside the web root; there are none in this request.",null,"Command injection appends OS commands with characters such as ; or |, whereas this payload manipulates SQL logic."],2],["cy19",0,1,"A web server log contains GET /download?file=../../../../etc/passwd. Which attack is this?",["Server-side request forgery","XML external entity injection","Cross-site request forgery","Directory traversal"],3,"Repeated ../ sequences try to climb out of the web root to read arbitrary files.","Objective 1.2",["SSRF makes the server request a URL of the attacker's choosing, such as an internal service; this request instead tries to climb the local file system with ../.","XXE abuses external entity declarations inside XML input; this is a simple GET parameter with no XML involved.","CSRF tricks a logged-in user's browser into sending an unwanted request; it does not use ../ sequences to read server files.",null],2],["cy20",0,1,"On a compromised host, an analyst finds a new service and a registry Run key that both launch the same unsigned executable at startup. What attacker goal do these serve?",["Lateral movement","Persistence","Reconnaissance","Exfiltration"],1,"Services and Run keys make malware survive reboots, which is persistence.","Objective 1.2",["Lateral movement is moving from one compromised host to others; a startup service and Run key keep the attacker on this same host.",null,"Reconnaissance is gathering information about targets; creating startup mechanisms happens after compromise and serves a different purpose.","Exfiltration is moving data out; services and Run keys only ensure the malware relaunches after a reboot."],2],["cy21",0,1,"A SOC receives thousands of alerts a day, and analysts close over 90% as false positives. What is the best first improvement?",["Disable the noisiest data sources in the SIEM","Forward all alerts to management","Tune the detection rules and suppress known benign activity","Lower the alert thresholds"],2,"Tuning rules to reduce false positives improves alert fidelity without losing visibility.","Objective 1.5",["Disabling noisy data sources removes visibility and could hide real attacks along with the false positives.","Forwarding alerts to management only spreads the noise; it does not improve alert quality or reduce the workload.",null,"Lowering thresholds makes rules fire more often, which would increase the number of false positives."],2],["cy22",0,1,"A regional hospital wants threat intelligence specific to healthcare, shared by peer organizations. Which source is best?",["The Information Sharing and Analysis Center (ISAC) for its sector","Dark web forums","A general antivirus vendor blog","Social media OSINT only"],0,"ISACs share sector-specific intelligence among member organizations.","Objective 1.4",[null,"Dark web forums can contain useful intelligence, but they are unvetted, risky to access and not organized around sharing among healthcare peers.","A general antivirus vendor blog covers broad threats and is not tailored to the healthcare sector or shared by peer organizations.","Social media OSINT is unverified and noisy, and relying on it alone gives no structured, sector-specific peer sharing."],1],["cy23",0,1,"An investigation finds that attackers used custom malware, stayed hidden in a defense contractor's network for 14 months and quietly stole engineering designs. Which threat actor type is most likely?",["Script kiddie","Hacktivist","Nation-state advanced persistent threat","Unintentional insider"],2,"Long dwell time, custom tooling and theft of defense IP are hallmarks of a well-resourced nation-state APT.","Objective 1.4",["Script kiddies use existing public tools and lack the resources and patience for custom malware and a 14-month covert operation.","Hacktivists usually seek publicity, such as defacements or leaks, rather than quietly stealing engineering designs for over a year.",null,"An unintentional insider causes harm by mistake; they would not deploy custom malware or run a long, stealthy theft campaign."],2],["cy24",0,1,"A paid intelligence feed delivers indicators that are months old, and most of the IPs are no longer malicious by the time they arrive. Which quality attribute is the feed failing on?",["Relevancy","Confidence level","Accuracy of attribution","Timeliness"],3,"Intelligence that arrives after it is useful fails on timeliness.","Objective 1.4",["Relevancy asks whether intelligence applies to your organization; the problem here is that the indicators arrive too late to be useful.","Confidence level describes how sure the source is about an assessment; stale indicators are a delivery-speed problem, not a confidence rating.","Attribution accuracy is about correctly identifying who is behind activity; the feed's issue is that its IPs are outdated by the time they arrive.",null],3],["cy25",0,1,"An analyst wants to review SSH login attempts on an Ubuntu server. Which file should they check first?",["/var/log/auth.log","/etc/shadow","/var/log/dmesg","/etc/hosts"],0,"On Debian and Ubuntu, authentication events including SSH logins are written to /var/log/auth.log.","Objective 1.3",[null,"/etc/shadow stores hashed account passwords, not a record of login attempts.","/var/log/dmesg holds kernel ring buffer messages such as hardware and driver events, not SSH authentication records.","/etc/hosts is a static hostname-to-IP mapping file and contains no log data at all."],1],["cy26",0,1,"Task Manager shows svchost.exe running from C:\\Users\\Public\\ rather than C:\\Windows\\System32. What is the most likely explanation?",["Normal Windows behavior","A driver update in progress","A corrupted page file","Malware masquerading as a legitimate system process"],3,"The real svchost.exe runs from System32; the same name in a user-writable folder is a masquerading indicator.","Objective 1.2",["Legitimate svchost.exe always runs from C:\\Windows\\System32 (or SysWOW64); running from C:\\Users\\Public is not normal behavior.","Driver updates are handled by Windows components in system folders and would not make svchost.exe run from a public user folder.","A corrupted page file causes memory errors or crashes; it cannot relocate a system binary to a user-writable directory.",null],3],["cy27",0,1,"A SOC starts using an AI assistant to summarize alerts and suggest next steps. Which practice is most appropriate?",["Let it close alerts automatically with no human review","Verify its conclusions against the underlying logs before acting","Paste full customer records into any public chatbot for better context","Turn off logging of the assistant's actions"],1,"AI output can be wrong, so analysts must validate it against source data; sensitive data should not go to unapproved tools.","Objective 1.5",["Letting AI close alerts with no human review risks dismissing real incidents, because AI output can be wrong or miss context.",null,"Pasting customer records into a public chatbot can leak sensitive data to an unapproved third party and violate privacy obligations.","Turning off logging removes accountability for what the assistant did; its actions should be logged like any other tool's."],2],["cy28",0,2,"A team wants the most accurate list of missing patches and weak configurations on its Windows servers, with the fewest false positives. Which scan should it run?",["Non-credentialed external scan","Credentialed scan","Passive network monitoring only","Ping sweep"],1,"Credentialed scans log in and read installed versions and settings directly, which improves accuracy.","Objective 2.1",["A non-credentialed external scan infers versions from banners and exposed services, so it misses many local patches and produces more false positives.",null,"Passive monitoring only sees what crosses the network and cannot read installed patches or local configuration settings.","A ping sweep only discovers which hosts are alive; it does not check for patches or configuration weaknesses."],2],["cy29",0,2,"Most employees use laptops that are rarely on the corporate network. Which scanning approach gives the best coverage?",["Agent-based scanning","Agentless scanning from headquarters","External perimeter scanning","Quarterly manual review"],0,"Agents scan locally and report in whenever the device has internet access, even off the corporate network.","Objective 2.1",[null,"Agentless scanning from headquarters can only reach devices while they are on the corporate network, which these laptops rarely are.","External perimeter scanning looks at internet-facing systems, not employee laptops sitting on home or public networks.","A quarterly manual review is slow, infrequent and error-prone, leaving laptops unassessed for long periods."],2],["cy30",0,2,"A manufacturer needs to find vulnerabilities on PLCs in a live production network where a crash would halt the line. What is the best approach?",["Aggressive active scan during production hours","Fuzzing the PLCs","Passive discovery and monitoring","Running exploit modules to confirm findings"],2,"OT devices can fail under active scanning, so passive techniques are preferred in production.","Objective 2.1",["Aggressive active scanning can crash fragile PLCs, and doing it during production hours risks halting the line.","Fuzzing deliberately sends malformed input to cause failures, which is exactly the kind of disruption the plant must avoid.",null,"Running exploit modules against live PLCs could crash or alter them, creating the safety and downtime risk the team wants to avoid."],3],["cy31",0,2,"A finding has the CVSS vector AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N. What does this tell the analyst?",["It requires physical access and a logged-in user","It can only be exploited from the local subnet by an admin","It needs a user to click a link but no privileges","It can be exploited remotely, with low complexity, no privileges and no user interaction"],3,"AV:N is network, AC:L low complexity, PR:N no privileges, UI:N no user interaction.","Objective 2.3",["AV:N means network, not physical (AV:P), and UI:N means no user interaction is needed, so neither physical access nor a logged-in user is required.","AV:N means exploitable across the network, not just the local subnet (AV:A), and PR:N means no admin or any other privileges are needed.","UI:N means no user interaction is required, so the attacker does not need anyone to click a link.",null],2],["cy32",0,2,"Two vulnerabilities both score CVSS 9.8. One is on an internet-facing VPN appliance and listed in the CISA KEV catalog; the other is on an isolated lab host. Which should be remediated first?",["The lab host, because it is easier to patch","Both at the same time in alphabetical order","The VPN appliance listed in the KEV catalog","Neither until the next quarterly window"],2,"Known exploitation plus internet exposure makes the VPN appliance the higher real-world risk.","Objective 2.3",["Ease of patching does not outweigh risk; an isolated lab host is far less exposed than an internet-facing appliance that is actively exploited.","Alphabetical order has nothing to do with risk; prioritization should consider exposure and known exploitation, not names.",null,"Waiting for a quarterly window leaves an internet-facing, actively exploited vulnerability open to attack for months."],3],["cy33",0,2,"A scanner flags an Apache vulnerability on a RHEL server based on the version banner. The admin shows that Red Hat backported the fix into the installed package. How should the finding be classified?",["True positive","False positive","False negative","True negative"],1,"The scanner reported a vulnerability that is not actually present, which is a false positive.","Objective 2.2",["A true positive would mean the vulnerability is really present, but the backported fix means the server is actually patched.",null,"A false negative is a real vulnerability the scanner failed to report; here the scanner reported one that does not exist.","A true negative is when the scanner correctly reports nothing; here it did raise a finding, just an incorrect one."],3],["cy34",0,2,"A legacy medical device runs an unsupported OS that the vendor will not allow to be patched. What is the best action?",["Segment the device and restrict access to it as a compensating control","Accept the risk with no documentation","Exclude the device from future scans","Disable logging to improve performance"],0,"When patching isn't possible, compensating controls such as segmentation and access restrictions reduce risk; the exception should be documented.","Objective 2.5",[null,"Accepting risk without documentation leaves no record of the decision, the owner or the review date, so the risk becomes invisible.","Excluding the device from scans hides the problem and removes visibility into new issues; it reduces no risk at all.","Disabling logging reduces visibility into attacks against an already vulnerable device, making the situation worse."],2],["cy35",0,2,"Which scoring system estimates the probability that a vulnerability will be exploited in the wild in the next 30 days?",["CVSS base score","EPSS","CWE","CPE"],1,"The Exploit Prediction Scoring System (EPSS) estimates exploitation likelihood; CVSS measures severity.","Objective 2.3",["The CVSS base score rates a vulnerability's technical severity; it does not estimate how likely exploitation is in the next 30 days.",null,"CWE is a catalog of software weakness types such as buffer overflows, not a scoring system for exploitation probability.","CPE is a naming scheme for identifying products and versions, not a way to score or predict exploitation."],1],["cy36",0,2,"A development team wants to find vulnerabilities by analyzing source code without running the application. Which technique should it use?",["Dynamic application security testing","Fuzzing","Static application security testing","Penetration testing"],2,"SAST analyzes code at rest; DAST and fuzzing test a running application.","Objective 2.2",["DAST tests a running application from the outside, so it requires executing the app rather than analyzing source code.","Fuzzing feeds unexpected input to a running program to trigger crashes; it does not analyze source code at rest.",null,"Penetration testing attacks a running system, typically with manual techniques, rather than analyzing source code without running it."],1],["cy37",0,2,"An nmap -sV scan of a network switch shows 23/tcp open telnet. What should the analyst recommend?",["Change the telnet port to 2323","Add a login banner","Block ICMP to the switch","Disable telnet and manage the switch over SSH"],3,"Telnet sends credentials in cleartext; SSH provides encrypted management.","Objective 2.2",["Moving telnet to port 2323 is security through obscurity; credentials still cross the network in cleartext.","A login banner provides legal notice but does nothing to encrypt the credentials and commands sent over telnet.","Blocking ICMP stops ping responses but leaves the cleartext telnet service on 23/tcp fully reachable.",null],2],["cy38",0,2,"A company wants continuous checks for misconfigurations such as publicly readable storage buckets and overly permissive security groups across its cloud accounts. Which tool fits best?",["Cloud security posture management (CSPM)","Web application firewall","Host-based intrusion detection","An OT protocol scanner"],0,"CSPM tools continuously assess cloud configurations against best practices.","Objective 2.2",[null,"A WAF filters HTTP traffic to web applications; it does not audit cloud account settings like bucket permissions or security groups.","A HIDS monitors activity on individual hosts and has no view of cloud control-plane configuration across accounts.","An OT protocol scanner is built for industrial devices such as PLCs, not for cloud storage and security group settings."],1],["cy39",0,2,"A critical patch is ready, but the business owner refuses to install it during quarter-end close because any downtime would delay financial reporting. What does this represent?",["A false positive","Risk transference","A zero-day vulnerability","An inhibitor to remediation due to business process interruption"],3,"Concern about disrupting business processes is a common inhibitor to remediation.","Objective 2.5",["A false positive is a reported vulnerability that is not real; here the vulnerability and patch are real but deployment is delayed.","Risk transference shifts impact to a third party, such as through insurance; delaying a patch for business reasons does not transfer risk.","A zero-day has no patch available; here a patch is ready and is only being held back for business reasons.",null],3],["cy40",0,2,"A DAST scan confirms stored cross-site scripting in a blog's comment field. What is the best mitigation?",["Enable TLS on the site","Implement account lockout","Apply output encoding and validate input","Use parameterized queries"],2,"Encoding output (and validating input) prevents injected script from executing in browsers. Parameterized queries address SQL injection.","Objective 2.4",["TLS encrypts traffic in transit, but the injected script is stored on the server and still runs in visitors' browsers over HTTPS.","Account lockout defends against password guessing and has no effect on script stored in a comment field.",null,"Parameterized queries prevent SQL injection by separating data from SQL code; they do not stop script from executing in browsers."],2],["cy41",0,2,"A code review finds a login query built by concatenating user input into a SQL string. What is the most effective fix?",["Hide error messages from users","Use parameterized queries (prepared statements)","Encode the output as HTML","Move the database to a separate server"],1,"Parameterized queries keep user input as data so it can't change the SQL statement.","Objective 2.4",["Hiding error messages makes SQL injection harder to discover, but the concatenated query is still exploitable, for example through blind techniques.",null,"HTML output encoding defends against XSS in the browser; it does not stop user input from changing a SQL statement.","Moving the database to another server changes where it runs, but the vulnerable query construction stays the same."],2],["cy42",0,2,"A web app fetches any URL a user supplies. A tester makes it request http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/ and gets cloud credentials back. Which vulnerability is this?",["Cross-site request forgery","XML external entity injection","Insecure direct object reference","Server-side request forgery"],3,"The server is tricked into requesting an internal resource (the cloud metadata service), which is SSRF.","Objective 2.4",["CSRF abuses a victim's browser to send authenticated requests; here the server itself fetched an internal URL on the attacker's behalf.","XXE uses malicious XML external entities to read files or make requests; this attack simply supplied a URL, with no XML involved.","IDOR is accessing another user's object by changing an identifier; here the server was made to reach an internal metadata service.",null],3],["cy43",0,2,"A user changes the URL from /account?id=1001 to /account?id=1002 and sees another customer's billing details. Which vulnerability is this?",["Insecure direct object reference","Buffer overflow","Race condition","Session fixation"],0,"The app exposes objects by ID without checking authorization, which is IDOR (broken access control).","Objective 2.4",[null,"A buffer overflow writes past the end of a memory buffer; changing an ID in a URL to view another record is an authorization flaw.","A race condition depends on the timing of concurrent operations; simply editing the account ID involves no timing issue.","Session fixation forces a victim to use a session ID the attacker knows; this user never took over anyone's session, they just changed an ID."],2],["cy44",0,2,"Which pair of operating system protections makes exploiting a buffer overflow harder?",["Address space layout randomization and data execution prevention","Input masking and autocomplete","Code signing and certificate pinning","SPF and DKIM"],0,"ASLR randomizes memory locations and DEP blocks code execution from data regions.","Objective 2.4",[null,"Input masking and autocomplete are user interface features for forms; they have no effect on memory corruption exploits.","Code signing verifies software publishers and certificate pinning protects TLS connections; neither hinders exploiting a buffer overflow in memory.","SPF and DKIM are email authentication mechanisms and have nothing to do with operating system memory protection."],1],["cy45",0,2,"After reviewing ransomware risk, leadership buys a cyber insurance policy. Which risk response is this?",["Acceptance","Avoidance","Mitigation","Transference"],3,"Insurance shifts financial impact to a third party, which is risk transference.","Objective 2.5",["Acceptance means acknowledging the risk and taking no further action; buying insurance is an action that shifts financial impact.","Avoidance means stopping the risky activity altogether; the organization still faces ransomware risk after buying insurance.","Mitigation reduces the likelihood or impact of the threat with controls such as backups or EDR; insurance does not make an attack less likely or less damaging.",null],2],["cy46",0,2,"Weekly vulnerability scans are slowing production databases during business hours. What is the best change?",["Stop scanning the databases","Switch to annual scans","Schedule scans off-hours and throttle scan intensity","Scan only from outside the firewall"],2,"Scheduling and throttling keeps coverage while reducing operational impact.","Objective 2.1",["Stopping database scans removes visibility into vulnerabilities on critical systems instead of fixing the performance problem.","Annual scans leave vulnerabilities undetected for up to a year, which is far too infrequent for production databases.",null,"Scanning only from outside the firewall misses most internal findings and does not address the timing of the scans."],2],["cy47",0,2,"A team needs to find known-vulnerable open-source libraries pulled into its application through dependencies. Which tool type is best?",["Software composition analysis","Static application security testing","Web application firewall","Data loss prevention"],0,"SCA inventories third-party components and matches them against known vulnerabilities.","Objective 2.2",[null,"SAST analyzes your own source code for flaws, but it is not designed to inventory third-party libraries and match them to known CVEs.","A WAF filters web traffic at runtime; it does not identify which vulnerable libraries are included in the application.","DLP prevents sensitive data from leaving the organization and has nothing to do with software dependencies."],1],["cy48",0,2,"A web scan reports that the session cookie lacks the Secure and HttpOnly flags. What is the best recommendation?",["Shorten the cookie name","Set the Secure and HttpOnly flags so the cookie is sent only over HTTPS and not readable by scripts","Store the session ID in the URL instead","Disable cookies site-wide"],1,"Secure stops the cookie going over plain HTTP; HttpOnly stops JavaScript (for example XSS) from reading it.","Objective 2.4",["The cookie's name has no effect on security; the missing Secure and HttpOnly flags are the actual weakness.",null,"Putting session IDs in the URL exposes them in logs, browser history and Referer headers, which is less secure than a cookie.","Disabling cookies site-wide would break session management; the fix is to set the correct flags on the session cookie."],2],["cy49",0,3,"An attacker emails a malicious attachment to targeted employees. Which Cyber Kill Chain phase is this?",["Reconnaissance","Weaponization","Delivery","Exploitation"],2,"Transmitting the weapon to the target (email, USB, web) is the delivery phase.","Objective 3.1",["Reconnaissance is researching targets, such as harvesting employee names; sending the attachment comes later.","Weaponization is building the malicious payload, such as pairing an exploit with a document, before it is sent to anyone.",null,"Exploitation happens when the payload actually runs on the victim's system, after the email has been delivered and opened."],2],["cy50",0,3,"An analyst documents the attacker's command-and-control servers and domains. In the Diamond Model, which feature do these belong to?",["Adversary","Capability","Victim","Infrastructure"],3,"C2 servers and domains are infrastructure; malware and exploits are capability.","Objective 3.1",["The adversary feature describes the attacker or group itself, not the servers and domains they use.","Capability covers the attacker's tools and techniques, such as malware and exploits, rather than the C2 servers they run on.","The victim feature is the targeted organization, person or asset, not the attacker's command-and-control systems.",null],2],["cy51",0,3,"An analyst wants to label observed credential dumping from LSASS with a standard technique ID to compare against detection coverage. Which framework should they use?",["MITRE ATT&CK","OWASP Testing Guide","NIST Cybersecurity Framework","Cyber Kill Chain"],0,"ATT&CK catalogs adversary techniques with IDs, such as T1003 for OS credential dumping.","Objective 3.1",[null,"The OWASP Testing Guide describes how to test web applications for vulnerabilities; it does not assign technique IDs to adversary behavior like credential dumping.","The NIST CSF organizes security program outcomes into functions such as Identify and Protect; it does not catalog specific attacker techniques with IDs.","The Cyber Kill Chain has seven broad phases with no technique IDs, so it cannot label credential dumping at the level needed to compare detection coverage."],2],["cy52",0,3,"After isolating an infected host, the team removes the malware, deletes the attacker's backdoor account and removes the scheduled task it created. Which phase is this?",["Containment","Eradication","Recovery","Preparation"],1,"Removing the threat and its persistence is eradication.","Objective 3.2",["Containment limits the spread, and the host was already isolated; removing the malware and persistence is the next phase.",null,"Recovery restores systems to normal operation and monitors them; it follows after the threat has been removed.","Preparation happens before an incident, building plans, tools and training, not cleaning up an active infection."],2],["cy53",0,3,"Ransomware is actively encrypting file shares from several workstations on one floor. What should the responders do first?",["Pay the ransom to stop the spread","Power off every server company-wide","Start restoring from backup onto the infected hosts","Isolate the affected hosts from the network"],3,"Isolation contains the spread while keeping evidence; restoring before containment risks reinfection.","Objective 3.2",["Paying the ransom does not stop encryption already in progress and gives no guarantee of decryption; containment is still needed.","Powering off every server company-wide causes massive disruption and destroys volatile evidence, when isolating the affected hosts would contain the spread.","Restoring onto hosts that are still infected lets the ransomware encrypt the restored data again; contain first.",null],3],["cy54",0,3,"A responder can collect only one source before a compromised server is shut down. Following the order of volatility, which should they collect?",["Hard disk image","Memory (RAM)","Backup tapes","Archived logs on the SIEM"],1,"RAM is lost at power-off, while disk, backups and remote logs persist.","Objective 3.2",["A disk image is important, but disk contents survive shutdown, so it can be collected after RAM.",null,"Backup tapes are archival and among the least volatile sources, so they are collected last if at all.","Archived logs on the SIEM are stored remotely and persist after the server is shut down."],2],["cy55",0,3,"What is the main purpose of a chain of custody form?",["To record who handled evidence, when and why, so its integrity can be shown","To encrypt evidence at rest","To list the incident's root cause","To notify customers of a breach"],0,"Chain of custody documents every transfer and access so evidence remains trustworthy and admissible.","Objective 3.2",[null,"Encrypting evidence protects its confidentiality, but that is a separate control from documenting who handled it.","The root cause belongs in the incident report or lessons-learned documentation, not in a chain of custody form.","Customer breach notification is a legal and communications task, unrelated to tracking evidence handling."],1],["cy56",0,3,"After creating a forensic image of a laptop drive, how should the investigator prove the image is an exact copy?",["Compare the file counts","Check the drive's serial number","Compare SHA-256 hashes of the original drive and the image","Open several files and compare them visually"],2,"Matching cryptographic hashes show the image is bit-for-bit identical to the source.","Objective 3.2",["Matching file counts does not prove every byte is identical, and it ignores slack space, unallocated space and deleted data.","The serial number identifies the physical drive but says nothing about whether the image contents match it.",null,"Visually comparing a few files is a small, subjective sample and cannot prove a bit-for-bit copy."],2],["cy57",0,3,"Two weeks after an incident closes, the team meets to review the timeline, identify what went wrong and update the playbooks. Which phase is this?",["Preparation","Detection and analysis","Containment","Post-incident activity"],3,"Lessons learned and improvements happen in post-incident activity.","Objective 3.3",["Preparation is building capabilities before incidents; while its outputs feed back into preparation, this review of a closed incident is post-incident activity.","Detection and analysis is identifying and scoping an active incident, not reviewing a closed one two weeks later.","Containment limits damage during an active incident; this incident is already closed.",null],1],["cy58",0,3,"The IR team runs a tabletop exercise walking through a simulated data breach with leadership. Which phase does this support?",["Preparation","Detection and analysis","Eradication","Recovery"],0,"Training and exercises before an incident happens are part of preparation.","Objective 3.3",[null,"Detection and analysis happens during a real incident, identifying and scoping it; a tabletop is practice done beforehand.","Eradication removes a real threat from systems; a tabletop simulates a scenario without touching any systems.","Recovery restores affected systems after a real incident; a tabletop exercise involves no actual restoration."],1],["cy59",0,3,"After confirming one infected laptop, an analyst searches EDR telemetry for the same malware hash and C2 domain across all endpoints. What is the main goal?",["Eradicate the malware","Determine the scope of the incident","Complete the lessons-learned report","Notify regulators"],1,"Hunting for the same IoCs across the environment establishes how far the incident has spread.","Objective 3.2",["Eradication removes the malware, which comes after you know every affected host; searching telemetry is about finding them first.",null,"The lessons-learned report is written after the incident closes, not while still searching for other infected endpoints.","Regulator notification is decided by legal and compliance; searching for IoCs on endpoints is a technical scoping step."],2],["cy60",0,3,"Legal counsel expects a lawsuit over an incident. What ensures relevant emails and logs are not deleted by normal retention policies?",["A legal hold","A data retention purge","A chain of custody form","A right-to-erasure request"],0,"A legal hold suspends normal deletion so relevant data is preserved.","Objective 3.2",[null,"A retention purge deletes data on schedule, which is exactly what must be stopped when litigation is expected.","Chain of custody documents who handled collected evidence; it does not stop routine retention policies from deleting emails and logs.","A right-to-erasure request asks an organization to delete personal data, the opposite of preserving it."],1],["cy61",0,3,"Before returning a server restored from backup to production after a ransomware attack, what should the team confirm?",["That the server has a new hostname","That the ransom note was deleted","That the backup predates the compromise and the entry point has been fixed","That antivirus was reinstalled from the same backup"],2,"Restoring a backup that contains the attacker's foothold, or leaving the entry point open, invites reinfection.","Objective 3.2",["A new hostname does nothing to ensure the server is free of the attacker's foothold or that the entry point is closed.","Deleting the ransom note is cosmetic; the malware or the vulnerability that let it in could still be present.",null,"Reinstalling antivirus from the same backup could reintroduce compromised files and does not address how the attacker got in."],3],["cy62",0,3,"After an incident, the team determines the attacker first got in through an unpatched VPN appliance. What activity is this?",["Containment","Triage","Root cause analysis","Threat modeling"],2,"Identifying the underlying cause of the incident is root cause analysis, done in post-incident activity.","Objective 3.3",["Containment limits the spread during an active incident; determining how the attacker got in afterward is a different activity.","Triage assesses and prioritizes alerts or incidents early on, not the post-incident determination of the initial entry point.",null,"Threat modeling proactively identifies potential threats to a system during design; it is not an investigation of an incident that already happened."],2],["cy63",0,3,"In the final stage of an intrusion, the attacker compresses and exfiltrates customer databases. Which Cyber Kill Chain phase is this?",["Installation","Actions on objectives","Command and control","Delivery"],1,"Achieving the attacker's goal, such as data theft, is actions on objectives.","Objective 3.1",["Installation is when malware establishes persistence on the victim host, which happens before the attacker achieves the goal.",null,"Command and control is the channel the attacker uses to operate the implant; stealing the data is the objective itself.","Delivery is sending the weapon to the target, such as by email, at the start of the intrusion."],2],["cy64",0,3,"Responders suspect a skilled attacker would notice an abrupt disconnect. They move the compromised host to a restricted, heavily monitored VLAN to limit damage while gathering intelligence. Which approach is this?",["Containment through segmentation","Eradication","Recovery","Reverse engineering"],0,"Segmentation limits what the host can reach while allowing monitoring, which is a containment strategy.","Objective 3.2",[null,"Eradication removes the threat from the host; here the attacker's access is deliberately kept under observation.","Recovery returns clean systems to normal operation; the host is still compromised and being watched.","Reverse engineering analyzes malware code to understand how it works; moving the host to a monitored VLAN is not code analysis."],3],["cy65",0,3,"During an incident, responders believe the attacker can read corporate email. How should the IR team coordinate?",["Continue using corporate email but mark messages urgent","Post updates in the company-wide chat","Wait until the attacker is removed before communicating","Use a pre-arranged out-of-band communication channel"],3,"If normal channels may be compromised, the IR plan should specify a separate, out-of-band channel.","Objective 3.3",["If the attacker can read corporate email, urgent messages are just as visible to them, tipping them off to the response.","A company-wide chat may also be compromised and exposes sensitive incident details to all employees.","Waiting to communicate stalls the response; the team needs a secure channel to coordinate now.",null],3],["cy66",0,3,"An analyst has a memory dump from a compromised Windows host and wants to list processes, network connections and injected code. Which tool fits?",["Nessus","Nmap","Burp Suite","Volatility"],3,"Volatility is a memory forensics framework with plugins such as pslist, netscan and malfind.","Objective 3.2",["Nessus is a vulnerability scanner for live systems; it cannot parse a memory dump to list processes or injected code.","Nmap scans networks for hosts and open ports and has no memory forensics capability.","Burp Suite is a web application testing proxy, not a tool for analyzing Windows memory images.",null],1],["cy67",0,3,"Which document gives responders step-by-step actions for one specific incident type, such as a phishing report?",["Acceptable use policy","Playbook","Business impact analysis","Service level agreement"],1,"Playbooks are scenario-specific procedures created during preparation.","Objective 3.3",["An acceptable use policy defines how employees may use company resources; it does not give responders step-by-step incident actions.",null,"A business impact analysis identifies critical functions and the impact of disruption, not specific response steps for phishing.","An SLA defines service commitments between parties, such as uptime or response times, not incident handling procedures."],2],["cy68",0,4,"The CISO asks for a quarterly vulnerability report for the board. What should it emphasize?",["Full raw scanner output for every host","Plugin IDs and port numbers","Business risk, trends over time and decisions or resources needed","Command-line remediation steps"],2,"Executives need risk in business terms and trends, not technical detail.","Objective 4.1",["Raw scanner output is far too technical and voluminous for board members to act on.","Plugin IDs and port numbers are technical details for the remediation team, not board-level information.",null,"Command-line remediation steps are for administrators, not executives making risk and funding decisions."],3],["cy69",0,4,"Which metric shows how long, on average, critical vulnerabilities stay open after they are found?",["Mean time to detect","Mean time to remediate","Number of scans completed","Scanner false positive rate"],1,"Mean time to remediate measures the average time from discovery to fix.","Objective 4.1",["Mean time to detect measures how quickly incidents or threats are identified, not how long vulnerabilities stay open after discovery.",null,"The number of scans completed measures scanning activity, not how quickly the findings get fixed.","The false positive rate measures scanner accuracy and says nothing about how long real vulnerabilities remain open."],1],["cy70",0,4,"Leadership wants to know the average time between an attacker's initial compromise and the SOC identifying it. Which metric is this?",["Recovery time objective","Recovery point objective","Mean time to respond","Mean time to detect"],3,"Mean time to detect measures how quickly incidents are identified.","Objective 4.2",["RTO is the target time to restore a system after an outage, not a measure of how long attackers go undetected.","RPO is the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time since the last backup, unrelated to detecting compromises.","Mean time to respond measures how long it takes to act after an incident is identified, not the time until it is identified.",null],2],["cy71",0,4,"An investigation shows an employee may have deliberately copied customer data before resigning. Besides legal, which stakeholder should be involved?",["Human resources","Marketing","Facilities","Vendor sales"],0,"Insider cases involve employment actions, so HR must be part of the communication.","Objective 4.2",[null,"Marketing handles promotion and brand messaging, not the employment actions an insider case may require.","Facilities manages buildings and physical space; it has no role in handling potential employee misconduct.","Vendor sales deals with external suppliers and is not involved in an internal investigation of an employee."],2],["cy72",0,4,"A breach exposed customer PII from several states. Who should determine whether regulators and customers must be notified?",["The tier 1 analyst who found it","Legal counsel and compliance","The help desk","Network engineering"],1,"Legal and compliance interpret notification laws and regulatory obligations.","Objective 4.2",["A tier 1 analyst detects and escalates incidents but lacks the legal authority and expertise to decide on regulatory notification.",null,"The help desk supports users with IT issues and has no role in interpreting breach notification laws.","Network engineering maintains infrastructure; notification decisions are legal and compliance matters."],2],["cy73",0,4,"A reporter calls a SOC analyst asking about a rumored breach. What should the analyst do?",["Confirm the details to prevent rumors","Deny any incident occurred","Refer the reporter to the designated public relations contact","Share the incident timeline off the record"],2,"Media communication goes through PR or a designated spokesperson so the organization speaks with one accurate voice.","Objective 4.2",["Confirming details to a reporter without authorization can spread inaccurate information and create legal exposure.","Denying an incident that may have occurred is dishonest and can badly damage trust if the breach is later confirmed.",null,"Nothing shared with a reporter is truly off the record for the organization; sharing the timeline leaks sensitive details without approval."],1],["cy74",0,4,"A system owner cannot patch a critical vulnerability for six months because of a vendor dependency. What should the vulnerability report record?",["Mark the finding as a false positive","Remove the finding from the report","Lower the CVSS score","A documented exception with compensating controls, an owner and an expiry date"],3,"Exceptions must be formally recorded with the controls in place and a review date so the risk stays visible.","Objective 4.1",["The vulnerability is real, so marking it a false positive would misrepresent the risk and hide it from oversight.","Removing the finding hides a known risk from decision-makers instead of tracking it until it is fixed.","The CVSS score reflects the vulnerability's severity; lowering it artificially does not reduce the actual risk. Compensating controls belong in the exception record instead.",null],3],["cy75",0,4,"What belongs in the executive summary of an incident report?",["A short account of what happened, the business impact, current status and key recommendations","Full packet captures","Every command the responders ran","Raw SIEM queries"],0,"The executive summary gives decision-makers the essentials; technical detail goes in later sections or appendices.","Objective 4.2",[null,"Full packet captures are raw technical evidence that belongs in an appendix or the technical section, not the executive summary.","Every command the responders ran is detailed technical information for the incident record, not a summary for decision-makers.","Raw SIEM queries are technical artifacts for analysts, not something executives need to read."],2],["cy76",0,4,"Monthly reports show the same OpenSSL vulnerability reappearing on newly built virtual machines even after each is patched. What should the analyst recommend?",["Stop scanning new VMs","Raise the severity threshold for reporting","Update the golden image or template used to build the VMs","Patch each VM faster after deployment"],2,"Recurrence on new builds points to a vulnerable base image; fixing it at the source stops recurrence.","Objective 4.1",["Stopping scans of new VMs hides the problem while every new build stays vulnerable.","Raising the reporting threshold hides the finding without fixing the vulnerable build source.",null,"Patching each VM faster still leaves every new VM vulnerable at deployment; fixing the image removes the flaw at its source."],3],["cy77",0,4,"Policy requires critical vulnerabilities to be fixed within 15 days. A dashboard shows 40% of criticals exceeded that window last quarter. What is this dashboard measuring?",["Mean time to detect","Compliance with remediation SLAs","Scanner coverage","Risk acceptance rate"],1,"Tracking fixes against a required time window is SLA compliance.","Objective 4.1",["Mean time to detect measures how fast threats are identified, not whether fixes met a required deadline.",null,"Scanner coverage measures how many assets are scanned, not whether findings were fixed within the policy window.","The risk acceptance rate tracks how many findings were formally accepted rather than fixed, not how many missed the 15-day deadline."],2],["cy78",0,4,"Why should the final incident report include the root cause and recommendations?",["To help prevent the same incident from happening again","To assign blame to individual employees","To satisfy the SIEM license terms","To replace the chain of custody documentation"],0,"Root cause and recommendations drive the fixes and control changes that prevent recurrence.","Objective 4.2",[null,"Incident reports should focus on fixing processes and controls; a blame-focused report discourages honest reporting.","SIEM licensing has no requirement for incident reports to include root cause or recommendations.","Chain of custody documentation tracks evidence handling and must be kept separately; the report does not replace it."],1],["cy79",0,4,"A tier 1 analyst confirms that a server is actively being exploited. According to a typical IR plan, what should happen next?",["Post the details on the company intranet","Email all employees about the attack","Wait until the end of the shift to write it up","Escalate to the incident response lead through the defined escalation path"],3,"Incident declaration and escalation should follow the IR plan's defined path so the right people act quickly.","Objective 4.2",["Posting details on the intranet spreads sensitive information widely and could alert an insider attacker; it is not an escalation step.","Emailing all employees may tip off the attacker and causes confusion; the IR lead must be engaged first.","Waiting until the end of the shift delays the response to active exploitation, allowing more damage.",null],3],["cy80",0,4,"The system administrators who will fix findings receive a vulnerability report. What do they need most?",["Only an overall risk grade","Affected hosts, CVE IDs, evidence and remediation steps","Projected impact on the stock price","A list of every scan ever run"],1,"Technical audiences need actionable details to remediate each finding.","Objective 4.1",["An overall risk grade alone does not tell administrators which systems to fix or how to fix them.",null,"Stock price impact is a business concern for executives, not information administrators need to remediate findings.","A list of every scan ever run is historical noise that does not help administrators fix the current findings."],2],["x1",0,1,"An email appears to come from ceo@example.com. The Return-Path is bounce@mailer-promo.biz and the Authentication-Results header shows dmarc=fail for example.com. What is the most likely conclusion?",["The CEO's own mailbox was compromised and used to send it","A mailing list rewrote the message while relaying it","The From address was forged; example.com did not authorize the sender","The recipient gateway is validating DKIM with the wrong selector and rejecting good mail"],2,"A DMARC failure with an unrelated Return-Path domain means the visible From domain was not authorized, which points to spoofing. Mail sent from the real, compromised CEO mailbox would normally pass SPF, DKIM and DMARC.","Domain 1: Email analysis",["Mail sent from the real CEO mailbox would pass DMARC for example.com, so a compromised mailbox does not fit.","List rewriting can break authentication, but nothing here suggests a list, and the unrelated Return-Path points to forgery.",null,"A DKIM selector is chosen by the signer, not the recipient; a receiver-side issue would not explain the foreign Return-Path."],2],["x2",0,1,"A domain controller logs Windows Security event ID 1102 at 02:14 with no matching change ticket. What should most concern the analyst?",["Someone cleared the Security audit log, possibly to hide activity","A new user account was created without approval","A scheduled task was registered to run at startup","An account was locked out after repeated failed logons from a remote workstation"],0,"Event ID 1102 records that the Security audit log was cleared, a common anti-forensics step after an intrusion. New account creation is event 4720, not 1102.","Domain 1: Windows Event IDs",[null,"Account creation is logged as event 4720, not 1102.","Scheduled task creation is logged as event 4698, not 1102.","Account lockouts are logged as event 4740, not 1102."],2],["x3",0,1,"A SIEM rule fires on System log event ID 7045 on a file server. The new entry's binary path is C:\\Windows\\Temp\\upd.exe. What does this most likely indicate?",["A user logged on interactively using cached domain credentials","A new service was installed, a common persistence or lateral movement method","The Windows Update service crashed and was restarted by the service control manager","Audit policy was changed to stop recording process creation"],1,"Event ID 7045 means a service was installed, and a service binary in a Temp folder is highly suspicious for persistence or remote execution tools. A crashed service being restarted does not create a new service installation event.","Domain 1: Host indicators",["Cached logons appear as Security logon events such as 4624, not as a System log 7045 entry.",null,"A restart of an existing service does not generate a new service installation event, and Windows Update does not run from Temp.","Audit policy changes are recorded in the Security log (event 4719), not as event 7045."],2],["x4",0,1,"Under a zero trust model, a remote employee on a managed laptop passed MFA this morning. She now requests access to a sensitive HR application. What should happen?",["Access is granted because she already completed MFA earlier today","Access is denied because remote users can never reach sensitive apps","Access is granted automatically because the laptop is company-managed and already connected through the corporate VPN","The request is evaluated again using identity, device posture and context"],3,"Zero trust never assumes trust from an earlier login or network location; every request is evaluated on identity, device health and context. Being on the VPN is exactly the implicit network trust that zero trust removes.","Domain 1: Zero trust",["Zero trust does not carry trust forward from an earlier login; each request is evaluated on its own.","Zero trust allows remote access when identity, device and context checks pass; it does not ban it outright.","Network location, including the VPN, is exactly the implicit trust zero trust is designed to remove.",null],3],["x5",0,1,"Security finds employees uploading company documents to dozens of unsanctioned SaaS file-sharing apps. Which control best provides visibility and policy enforcement over this cloud app use?",["Cloud access security broker","Host-based intrusion prevention system","Network access control with 802.1X","Web application firewall in front of the company website"],0,"A CASB discovers shadow IT cloud apps and enforces policies such as blocking uploads or applying DLP. A WAF protects the company's own web apps from inbound attacks, not outbound SaaS use.","Domain 1: CASB",[null,"A HIPS protects a single host from exploits; it has no view of which SaaS apps users upload to.","802.1X controls which devices join the LAN, not what data users send to cloud services.","A WAF protects the company's own web apps from inbound attacks, not outbound use of third-party SaaS."],1],["x6",0,1,"Employees must log in to a partner's web portal with their existing corporate credentials, and the partner must never store their passwords. Which approach fits best?",["Syncing corporate password hashes into the partner's directory","Federated identity using SAML or OpenID Connect","Creating shared service accounts at the partner","A password manager with a shared vault for the portal"],1,"Federation lets the corporate identity provider authenticate users and send the partner a signed assertion, so the partner never sees passwords. Syncing hashes still gives the partner credential material to protect and lose.","Domain 1: Federation and SSO",["Syncing hashes gives the partner credential material to store and protect, which the requirement forbids.",null,"Shared accounts remove individual accountability and still require the partner to hold passwords.","A shared vault still means passwords for the portal exist and are shared, with no link to corporate identity."],1],["x7",0,1,"EDR on a Kubernetes node shows a web application container spawning /bin/sh and running curl to download a script from an unfamiliar IP address. What is the best interpretation?",["Normal behavior, because containers routinely pull updates at runtime","A liveness probe restarting the pod after a failed health check","Likely compromise of the container, such as remote code execution in the app","Image layer caching and garbage collection performed by the container runtime on the node"],2,"Containers are meant to be immutable, so a web app spawning a shell and fetching scripts is a strong sign of exploitation. Updates are delivered by rebuilding and redeploying images, not by downloading scripts inside a running container.","Domain 1: Containers",["Containers are meant to be immutable; updates come from new images, not scripts fetched inside a running container.","Liveness probes run a configured check and restart pods; they do not spawn shells that download scripts from unknown IPs.",null,"Layer caching happens in the runtime on the node, not as a shell process inside the application container."],2],["x8",0,1,"A company runs its public API on serverless functions, so analysts cannot install EDR agents. What is the best source of security telemetry for these workloads?",["Packet captures from a SPAN port on the office core switch","Host-based firewall logs from the servers running the functions","Weekly credentialed vulnerability scans of the function hosts","The cloud provider's function execution and API audit logs, sent to the SIEM"],3,"With serverless, the provider manages the hosts, so visibility comes from the platform's execution, invocation and control-plane audit logs. The customer has no access to the underlying servers, so host firewall logs and host scans are not available.","Domain 1: Serverless and logging",["Serverless traffic runs inside the provider's network and never crosses an office switch.","The customer has no access to the servers that run serverless functions, so there are no host firewall logs to collect.","The provider manages the function hosts; the customer cannot scan them with credentials.",null],2],["x9",0,1,"A hunting query flags a host whose HTTPS connections to one domain occur every 300 seconds, plus or minus about 10%. The domain was registered five days ago. What is the best next step?",["Identify the process making the connections and check the domain's reputation","Close the alert, because traffic protected by HTTPS can be trusted","Block all HTTPS traffic from the entire subnet until the cause is found","Reimage every host in the subnet immediately so the beaconing stops before it spreads further"],0,"Regular intervals with jitter to a newly registered domain look like beaconing, so the analyst should confirm which process is responsible and enrich the domain. Encryption says nothing about whether the destination is benign, and blanket blocking or reimaging is premature before scoping.","Domain 1: Network indicators",[null,"HTTPS only encrypts the channel; malware routinely uses HTTPS for command and control.","Blocking all HTTPS for a subnet is a disruptive overreaction before the activity is even confirmed or scoped.","Reimaging every host before identifying the cause destroys evidence and may not even include the right systems."],3],["x10",0,1,"Several users on one floor suddenly receive addresses in 192.168.50.0/24 with an unfamiliar default gateway, while the corporate scope is 10.20.0.0/16. What is the most likely cause?",["An expired lease on the corporate DHCP server","A rogue DHCP server on that network segment","DNS cache poisoning on the local resolver","An IP address conflict with a network printer"],1,"Clients receiving an unknown scope and gateway means another DHCP server is answering, which can be used to redirect traffic through an attacker. An expired lease would cause renewal from the corporate server, not addresses from a different range.","Domain 1: Rogue devices",["An expired lease leads to renewal from the corporate server, not to addresses from an unknown range and gateway.",null,"DNS poisoning changes name resolution answers; it does not hand out IP addresses or gateways.","An IP conflict affects single addresses in the existing scope, not a whole floor moving to a new subnet."],2],["x11",0,1,"Firewall logs show one internal host sending TCP SYN packets to ports 1-1024 on 200 internal addresses within two minutes, with almost no completed handshakes. What does this indicate?",["A distributed denial-of-service attack against the firewall","Normal DHCP lease renewals from the host","Network scanning from a possibly compromised host","A broadcast storm caused by a switching loop"],2,"Many SYNs to sequential ports across many hosts with few completed handshakes is the signature of a SYN port scan, often reconnaissance for lateral movement. A DDoS would target one victim with high volume from many sources, not sweep internal hosts.","Domain 1: Network indicators",["A DDoS floods one target with high volume from many sources, not SYNs from one host across 200 internal addresses.","DHCP renewals use UDP to the DHCP server, not TCP SYNs to many ports on many hosts.",null,"A broadcast storm is layer 2 flooding of broadcast frames, not targeted TCP connection attempts."],1],["x12",0,1,"Which Windows Security event ID should an analyst monitor to detect creation of a new scheduled task, a common persistence method?",["4625","4720","4740","4698"],3,"Event ID 4698 records that a scheduled task was created. 4720 is tempting because it also records something new being created, but it logs a new user account, not a task.","Domain 1: Windows Event IDs",["4625 records failed logons, not scheduled task creation.","4720 records creation of a new user account, not a scheduled task.","4740 records an account lockout, not a scheduled task.",null],1],["x13",0,1,"An analyst wants the full command line, parent process and file hash for every process started on endpoints. Which log source provides this?",["Sysmon Event ID 1","Windows Security Event ID 4624","Sysmon Event ID 3","Windows System Event ID 6005"],0,"Sysmon Event ID 1 is process creation and includes the command line, parent process and hashes. Sysmon Event ID 3 records network connections, not process starts.","Domain 1: Sysmon",[null,"4624 records successful logons and has no process command lines or file hashes.","Sysmon Event ID 3 records network connections, not process creation details.","System event 6005 only notes that the Event Log service started, usually at boot."],1],["x14",0,1,"To reduce SIEM licensing costs, an administrator sets all application servers to log only ERROR and above. What is the main security drawback?",["Logs will be written in a format the SIEM cannot parse","Successful logins and other routine events needed for investigations will be lost","Time synchronization with NTP will stop working on the servers","Error-level entries will begin to include user passwords"],1,"Security investigations depend on informational events such as successful authentications and configuration changes, which are dropped when only errors are logged. The log level changes what is recorded, not the format the SIEM parses.","Domain 1: Log levels",["The log level changes which events are recorded, not the format the SIEM receives.",null,"Log verbosity has no effect on NTP time synchronization.","Changing the log level does not cause passwords to be logged; that is a separate coding flaw."],3],["x15",0,1,"The SOC deploys a decoy file server with enticing share names on the internal network. No legitimate user has any reason to access it. What is the main value of this decoy?",["It holds backups that ransomware cannot encrypt","It lets the SOC hack back against attacker infrastructure","Any connection to it is a high-fidelity sign of scanning or lateral movement","It removes the need for endpoint detection and logging on the real production file servers"],2,"Because nobody should touch a honeypot, any interaction is a strong, low-noise signal of malicious activity. Active defense of this kind stays inside your own network; hacking back against attacker systems is not what it provides.","Domain 1: Active defense and honeypots",["A decoy server is not a protected backup store and offers no ransomware resilience.","Honeypots are passive detection inside your own network; attacking attacker systems is a separate and legally risky act.",null,"A single decoy detects some activity but cannot replace endpoint detection on real systems."],2],["x16",0,1,"A retailer's threat feed is full of indicators about attacks on industrial control systems in the energy sector. The retailer runs no industrial control systems. Which intelligence quality is lacking?",["Timeliness","Accuracy","Completeness","Relevancy"],3,"Intelligence has to apply to the organization's own environment and threats; ICS data does not apply to a retailer. The indicators may be current and accurate, so timeliness and accuracy are not the problem.","Domain 1: Threat intelligence confidence",["Nothing suggests the indicators are old; the problem is that they do not apply to a retailer.","The data may be perfectly accurate about ICS attacks; accuracy is not the issue.","Completeness is about missing details, but here the whole topic is outside the retailer's environment.",null],2],["x17",0,1,"An analyst must share a description of a campaign, including indicators, attack patterns and the threat actor, in a structured, machine-readable format. Which standard defines this format?",["STIX","TAXII","SAML","Syslog"],0,"STIX defines the structured language for describing threat intelligence objects such as indicators, attack patterns and actors. TAXII is the transport used to exchange STIX content, not the content format itself.","Domain 1: STIX/TAXII",[null,"TAXII is the transport protocol for exchanging threat intel, not the data format describing it.","SAML exchanges authentication assertions, not threat intelligence.","Syslog carries log messages and has no model for campaigns, actors or attack patterns."],1],["x18",0,1,"A phishing email links to a domain that closely resembles the company's bank. What quick check best helps decide whether the domain is likely malicious?",["Open the link from a production workstation to see the page","Look up its WHOIS registration date and reputation in threat intelligence services","Ping the domain to confirm the server is reachable","Reply to the sender and ask whether the link is legitimate"],1,"A lookalike domain registered very recently or with poor reputation is a strong phishing signal, and these lookups do not touch the site. Opening it on a production workstation risks infection or credential theft.","Domain 1: Reputation and WHOIS lookups",["Opening a suspected phishing site from a production workstation risks infection or credential capture.",null,"A reachable server says nothing about whether it is malicious.","Replying confirms the mailbox is active and the attacker will simply claim the link is safe."],2],["x19",0,1,"An analyst needs a full capture of traffic between suspected infected host 10.1.1.25 and the internet, saved for later analysis in Wireshark. Which approach fits best?",["Run nmap -sS against 10.1.1.25 and save the output","Export NetFlow records for 10.1.1.25 from the edge router","Run tcpdump with a host 10.1.1.25 filter and write the output to a pcap file","Review the DHCP lease history and DNS query logs recorded for 10.1.1.25 over the past week"],2,"tcpdump with a host filter and the write option captures full packets into a pcap that Wireshark can open. NetFlow is tempting but only records flow metadata such as addresses, ports and byte counts, not payloads.","Domain 1: Wireshark/tcpdump",["nmap scans the host's ports; it does not capture that host's traffic to the internet.","NetFlow records only flow metadata such as addresses and byte counts, not full packets for Wireshark.",null,"DHCP lease history shows address assignments, not any of the traffic content."],2],["x20",0,1,"A SOC wants a SOAR playbook that disables user accounts whenever an impossible-travel alert fires. The alert's false positive rate is unknown. What is the best way to introduce this automation?",["Run the playbook against every account at once to test it","Delete the alert, because impossible-travel detections often misfire","Have the playbook delete accounts as well, to prevent reuse","Start with analyst approval before each disable, then automate fully once accuracy is known"],3,"A human-approval step lets the team measure false positives before automated actions can lock out legitimate users. Removing the alert entirely throws away a useful detection instead of tuning it.","Domain 1: Automation and orchestration",["Running an untested disabling playbook against every account could lock out the whole organization.","Removing the alert discards a useful detection instead of measuring and tuning it.","Deleting accounts is irreversible and far too drastic for an alert with an unknown false positive rate.",null],3],["x21",0,2,"A company wants to know which vulnerabilities an internet-based attacker could see on its public-facing servers. Which scan best answers this?",["An external scan from outside the perimeter","An internal credentialed scan from the server VLAN","An agent-based scan on each server","A passive scan from the internal core switch"],0,"An external scan shows exactly what is exposed through the firewall to an outside attacker. An internal credentialed scan finds more issues, but many of them cannot be reached from the internet, so it does not answer this question.","Domain 2: Internal vs external scanning",[null,"An internal credentialed scan reports many issues that cannot be reached from the internet.","Agents report what is installed on each host, not what is exposed through the perimeter.","Passive internal monitoring sees internal traffic and cannot show an outside attacker's view."],1],["x22",0,2,"A penetration tester exploits SQL injection in an application that last week's vulnerability scan reported as clean. How should the scan result be classified?",["True negative","False negative","False positive","True positive"],1,"The scanner reported no issue when a real vulnerability existed, which is a false negative. A false positive is the opposite: a reported issue that does not actually exist.","Domain 2: Validating results",["A true negative means no issue was reported and none existed, but a real flaw existed here.",null,"A false positive is a reported issue that does not exist; here nothing was reported.","A true positive would require the scanner to have reported the flaw, which it did not."],2],["x23",0,2,"A CVSS v3.1 base vector for a hypervisor flaw includes S:C. What does this mean?",["The attacker needs physical access to the host","The organization has customized the score with environmental metrics","Exploiting the flaw can affect resources beyond the vulnerable component's security scope","The vulnerability is confirmed to be exploited in the wild and has been added to a public catalog"],2,"Scope Changed means an exploit can impact components beyond the vulnerable one, such as escaping a VM to the host, which raises the score. Exploitation in the wild is tracked by sources such as the KEV catalog, not by the Scope metric.","Domain 2: CVSS base metrics",["Physical access is shown by AV:P in the attack vector, not by the Scope metric.","Environmental customization uses separate environmental metrics; S:C is part of the base vector.",null,"CVSS base vectors do not record exploitation in the wild; sources such as KEV track that."],2],["x24",0,2,"A firmware flaw has CVSS v3.1 attack vector AV:P. Compared with an otherwise identical flaw rated AV:N, how should it generally be prioritized?",["Higher, because it can be exploited from anywhere on the internet","The same, because the attack vector does not change the score","Higher, because it affects only systems in the DMZ","Lower, because the attacker needs physical access to the device"],3,"AV:P means physical access is required, which sharply limits who can exploit it and lowers the score versus a network vector. Exploitation from anywhere on the internet describes AV:N, not AV:P.","Domain 2: CVSS base metrics",["Exploitation from anywhere on the internet is what AV:N means, not AV:P.","Attack vector is a base metric and does change the score; AV:P lowers it relative to AV:N.","Attack vector values describe how access is gained, not network zones such as a DMZ.",null],2],["x25",0,2,"Testers send large volumes of malformed and random input to a file-parsing service to find crashes that may reveal memory corruption. Which technique is this?",["Fuzzing","Static code analysis","Software composition analysis","Credentialed scanning"],0,"Fuzzing feeds unexpected input to running code to trigger crashes and errors that often point to memory safety bugs. Static analysis reviews source code without running it, so it sends no input at all.","Domain 2: Fuzzing",[null,"Static analysis reviews code without running it, so it sends no input to the service.","SCA identifies known-vulnerable third-party components rather than testing input handling.","Credentialed scanning checks installed versions and settings, not how a parser handles malformed input."],1],["x26",0,2,"A team must test a third-party web application, for which it has no source code, for issues such as XSS while the app runs in staging. Which technique fits?",["Static application security testing","Dynamic application security testing","Software composition analysis","Code signing and binary integrity verification"],1,"DAST probes a running application from the outside and needs no source code. SAST requires access to the source code, which the team does not have.","Domain 2: SAST and DAST",["SAST needs the source code, which the team does not have.",null,"SCA inventories third-party libraries from code or manifests and does not test for XSS in a running app.","Code signing checks confirm publisher and integrity, not application vulnerabilities."],2],["x27",0,2,"The same CVSS 8.1 vulnerability appears on a public e-commerce server that handles card data and on an internal test server with no sensitive data. Neither is in the KEV catalog. Which should be remediated first?",["The test server, because it is simpler to patch","Both at the same time, because the CVSS scores are identical","The e-commerce server, because asset value and exposure raise its risk","Neither, because the vulnerability is not in the KEV catalog"],2,"CVSS base scores ignore business context; exposure to the internet and sensitive data make the e-commerce server the higher risk. Absence from KEV lowers urgency somewhat but does not mean a high-severity flaw can be ignored.","Domain 2: Prioritization",["Ease of patching does not make a low-value test server the higher risk.","CVSS base scores ignore asset value and exposure, which differ greatly here.",null,"KEV absence lowers urgency but does not mean a high-severity flaw on a card-data server can be ignored."],3],["x28",0,2,"A scanner reports that /search?q=<script>alert(1)</script> returns the script unescaped in the results page. What is the vulnerability and the best fix?",["SQL injection; rewrite the search feature to use parameterized queries","Server-side request forgery; allowlist outbound URLs","Stored XSS; delete the affected database records","Reflected XSS; apply context-aware output encoding to the q parameter"],3,"Input echoed straight back into the response is reflected XSS, and encoding output for its HTML context stops the browser running it. It is not stored XSS because nothing is saved; the payload comes back in the same request.","Domain 2: Common software vulnerabilities",["The payload runs in the browser, not the database, so this is not SQL injection.","SSRF makes the server fetch URLs; here a script is reflected back to the user's browser.","Nothing is stored; the payload is reflected in the same response, and deleting records would not fix the code.",null],2],["x29",0,2,"A regular user finds that browsing directly to /admin/users lets them view and edit every account, even though the menu hides that link. Which vulnerability is this?",["Broken access control due to missing server-side authorization","Cross-site scripting in the admin interface caused by unescaped menu output","Insecure cookie configuration","Buffer overflow in the web server"],0,"Hiding a link in the interface is not authorization; the server must check the user's role on every request. Cookie flags protect how session cookies are sent, not which pages a logged-in user may reach.","Domain 2: Broken access control",[null,"XSS runs attacker scripts in a victim's browser; here the user simply reaches a page they should not.","Cookie flags affect how cookies are sent, not whether the server checks a user's role.","A buffer overflow is a memory corruption flaw, not a missing authorization check."],1],["x30",0,2,"A critical patch must be applied to production database servers. In a mature environment, what should happen before it is deployed?",["Install it right away on every server during business hours without testing","Test it, get change approval and schedule it with a rollback plan","Wait until the next annual audit to approve it","Let each database owner decide privately whether to install it"],1,"Change management means testing, approval and a maintenance window with rollback, which balances speed with stability. Installing untested patches in business hours risks outages that can be worse than the vulnerability.","Domain 2: Change management",["Untested patches on every server in business hours risk outages worse than the vulnerability.",null,"Waiting for an annual audit leaves a critical flaw open for far too long.","Leaving it to individual owners bypasses change control and leads to inconsistent patching."],2],["x31",0,2,"A legacy FTP service used for partner file exchange cannot be secured. Leadership shuts it down and stops offering that exchange method entirely. Which risk response is this?",["Acceptance","Transference","Avoidance","Mitigation"],2,"Eliminating the activity that creates the risk is avoidance. Mitigation would keep the service running but add controls to reduce the risk.","Domain 2: Risk management",["Acceptance would keep the service running and live with the risk.","Transference shifts the financial impact to another party, such as an insurer.",null,"Mitigation would keep the service but add controls to reduce the risk."],1],["x32",0,2,"A low-severity finding on an internal kiosk would cost far more to fix than any likely loss. What is the appropriate way to handle it?",["Remove it from all future scan reports and move on without telling anyone","Transfer it by buying insurance specifically for the kiosk","Mark it as a false positive so it stops appearing","Formally accept the risk with documented approval from the risk owner"],3,"Risk acceptance is valid when it is documented and approved by someone with authority, and reviewed later. Marking a real finding as a false positive misrepresents the scan result and hides it from future review.","Domain 2: Risk acceptance and exceptions",["Silently removing a real finding hides it with no approval or review.","Buying insurance for a low-value kiosk costs more than the risk and does not fit the scenario.","The finding is real, so marking it as a false positive falsifies the record.",null],2],["x33",0,2,"A hosting provider manages a company's web servers under contract. A critical flaw is found, but the agreement says the provider patches only in its scheduled quarterly window. What inhibitor to remediation is this?",["Contractual service level agreement terms","Degraded functionality expected after patching the web servers","A legacy system without vendor support","Lack of a credentialed scan"],0,"The contract with the third party limits when fixes can be applied, which is an SLA or MOU inhibitor. The system is still supported and patchable, so it is not a legacy system problem.","Domain 2: Inhibitors to remediation",[null,"Nothing says the patch breaks functionality; the delay comes from the contract.","The servers are supported and patchable, just not on the company's schedule.","The flaw has already been found, so scan type is not what is blocking the fix."],3],["x34",0,2,"A Nessus report lists a critical flaw in a service on port 8443, but the server owner says nothing listens on that port. What should the analyst do first?",["Close the finding as a false positive based on the owner's statement","Validate it, for example by checking listening ports on the host and rescanning","Escalate it as a confirmed breach","Remove port 8443 from all future scan policies"],1,"Findings should be validated with evidence before they are closed or escalated. Closing on the owner's word alone could hide a real, forgotten service.","Domain 2: Validating results",["The owner may not know about the service; closing without evidence can hide a real risk.",null,"One unvalidated scan finding is not evidence of a breach.","Excluding the port hides any future findings there instead of resolving this one."],2],["x35",0,2,"A web app passes a user-supplied filename into a shell command. A tester appends ; cat /etc/passwd to the filename and sees the file contents. What is the best remediation?",["Encode the page output as HTML entities","Add a WAF rule that blocks the word passwd","Call the library API directly instead of a shell and allowlist valid filenames","Serve the application only over HTTPS"],2,"This is OS command injection; removing the shell call and strictly validating input removes the injection point. A WAF keyword rule is easily bypassed with other commands and does not fix the code.","Domain 2: Recommending controls",["HTML encoding prevents XSS in the page, not shell commands built on the server.","Blocking one word is trivially bypassed with other commands and leaves the injection in place.",null,"HTTPS protects data in transit and does nothing to stop command injection."],3],["x36",0,2,"Most of a company's cloud workloads are instances that live only a few hours. Weekly network scans miss most of them. What is the best approach?",["Increase network scans to once a day","Stop assessing them, because short-lived instances carry little risk","Rely on the provider's shared responsibility to cover the instances","Scan the machine images in the build pipeline and use agent-based or cloud-native assessment"],3,"Ephemeral instances are best secured by scanning the images they are built from and using continuous assessment. Daily scans still miss instances that come and go within hours.","Domain 2: Cloud scanning",["Instances that live for hours can still be missed by a scan that runs once a day.","Short-lived instances can still be exploited and are rebuilt from the same vulnerable images.","Under shared responsibility, the customer is still responsible for the OS and software in its instances.",null],3],["x37",0,3,"In the Diamond Model, a custom credential-stealing malware family used during an intrusion belongs to which feature?",["Capability","Infrastructure","Adversary","Victim"],0,"Capability covers the tools and techniques an adversary uses, including malware. Infrastructure is tempting, but it refers to the servers, domains and addresses used to deliver or control the capability.","Domain 3: Diamond Model",[null,"Infrastructure is the servers, domains and IPs used to deliver or control the malware, not the malware itself.","The adversary is the actor behind the intrusion, not the tool it uses.","The victim is the targeted organization or asset."],1],["x38",0,3,"In MITRE ATT&CK, what is the difference between a tactic and a technique?",["A tactic is a specific tool; a technique is a malware family","A tactic is the adversary's goal; a technique is how it is achieved","A tactic applies to cloud systems; a technique applies to endpoints","A tactic is a detection rule; a technique is its response action"],1,"Tactics are the why, such as Credential Access, and techniques are the how, such as OS Credential Dumping. Tools and malware are listed as software in ATT&CK, not as tactics.","Domain 3: MITRE ATT&CK",["Tools and malware are listed as software in ATT&CK, not as tactics or techniques.",null,"ATT&CK has separate matrices by platform; tactics and techniques exist in all of them.","Detections and responses are mapped to techniques; they are not what tactics and techniques are."],1],["x39",0,3,"A team wants a community methodology specifically for testing web application security, with test cases for authentication, session management and input validation. Which should it use?",["OSSTMM","Cyber Kill Chain intrusion model","OWASP Web Security Testing Guide","Diamond Model"],2,"The OWASP testing guide is focused on web application testing and organized by areas such as authentication and session management. OSSTMM is a broader security testing methodology covering many channels, not specifically web apps.","Domain 3: OWASP Testing Guide",["OSSTMM is a broad methodology across many channels, not specifically web app test cases.","The Kill Chain describes attack stages; it is not a testing methodology.",null,"The Diamond Model is for analyzing intrusions, not for testing applications."],1],["x40",0,3,"Two alerts arrive together: malware detected and quarantined on a lobby kiosk, and a confirmed large outbound transfer from a database holding customer PII. How should the analyst triage them?",["Handle them in the order they arrived","Prioritize the kiosk, because the malware was identified by name","Wait for a third alert to confirm a pattern","Prioritize the database, because sensitive data and active impact make it more severe"],3,"Severity depends on impact and data sensitivity; an active exfiltration of PII outranks malware that was already quarantined. A named detection does not make an incident more severe, and the kiosk threat is already contained.","Domain 3: Severity and triage",["Arrival order ignores impact; severity should drive the order.","The kiosk malware is already quarantined, so it is lower impact than active PII exfiltration.","Waiting lets a confirmed data transfer continue unchecked.",null],3],["x41",0,3,"Before imaging a suspect's hard drive, what should an investigator use to make sure the original drive is not modified?",["A hardware write blocker","A disk defragmentation tool","A full antivirus scan","A secure file-shredding utility"],0,"A write blocker allows reading while preventing any writes to the evidence drive. An antivirus scan would read and possibly quarantine or change files, altering the evidence.","Domain 3: Evidence acquisition",[null,"Defragmenting rewrites the drive and destroys evidence such as deleted file remnants.","A scan may quarantine or modify files and updates access times on the evidence.","Shredding tools destroy data; they are the opposite of preservation."],1],["x42",0,3,"An examiner has a forensic disk image and wants a free GUI tool to browse the file system, recover deleted files and build a timeline. Which tool fits?",["Nmap","Autopsy","Volatility","OpenVAS"],1,"Autopsy is a free disk forensics platform for browsing images, carving deleted files and building timelines. Volatility is a forensics tool too, but it analyzes memory dumps rather than disk images.","Domain 3: Disk analysis tools",["Nmap scans networks for hosts and ports; it does not analyze disk images.",null,"Volatility analyzes memory dumps, not file systems in disk images.","OpenVAS is a vulnerability scanner, not a forensics tool."],1],["x43",0,3,"A server is confirmed to be infected with a kernel-level rootkit. What is the most reliable eradication approach?",["Run a second antivirus product and delete whatever it finds","Delete the rootkit's known files and registry keys, then reboot the server twice","Rebuild it from a known-good image and restore data from clean backups","Change the admin password and keep monitoring"],2,"A rootkit can hide itself from tools on the infected system, so only a rebuild from trusted media gives confidence it is gone. Deleting known files misses components the rootkit may be hiding.","Domain 3: Eradication and recovery",["A rootkit can hide from tools running on the infected OS, so another scanner may miss it.","Deleting known files misses hidden components, and the rootkit may survive the reboot.",null,"Changing a password does not remove malicious code running in the kernel."],3],["x44",0,3,"Responders find a skilled intruder on one server and suspect other footholds exist. Why might they monitor briefly before containing that server?",["Monitoring means the incident does not have to be documented","Disconnecting a server always destroys the evidence on its disk","Attackers never move to other systems once they are detected","Acting too early can tip off the attacker while other footholds stay active"],3,"Watching briefly can reveal the full scope so all footholds are removed together, as long as the risk of further damage is acceptable. Disconnecting a server does not destroy disk evidence; it mainly affects volatile data such as memory and connections.","Domain 3: Containment strategies",["Documentation is required whether the team monitors or contains.","Disconnecting mainly loses volatile data such as live connections; disk evidence stays intact.","Detected attackers often move faster or switch to other footholds, which is the risk here.",null],3],["x45",0,3,"After malware is removed from several web servers, attackers regain access within days through the same vulnerable plugin. Which step was missed?",["Fixing the initial entry point before recovery","Capturing memory images from the servers","Notifying law enforcement","Updating the executive summary"],0,"Eradication and recovery must close the vulnerability that let the attacker in, or they can simply return. Memory captures help investigation but do nothing to stop re-entry.","Domain 3: Eradication and recovery",[null,"Memory images help investigation but do not stop attackers using the same plugin again.","Law enforcement contact may be appropriate but does not close the vulnerability.","Reporting updates do nothing to prevent re-entry."],2],["x46",0,3,"An attacker builds a malicious PDF that exploits a reader flaw and embeds a backdoor, before sending anything to the target. Which Cyber Kill Chain phase is this?",["Reconnaissance","Weaponization","Delivery","Installation"],1,"Weaponization is pairing an exploit with a payload to create a deliverable. Delivery is tempting, but it only starts when the file is actually sent to the victim.","Domain 3: Cyber Kill Chain",["Reconnaissance is gathering information about the target before building anything.",null,"Delivery begins when the file is sent to the victim, which has not happened yet.","Installation is when the backdoor is placed on the victim's system after exploitation."],1],["x47",0,3,"Which sequence follows the order of volatility from most to least volatile?",["Disk, RAM, CPU cache, archival backups","RAM, local disk, CPU registers, then remote syslog server data","CPU registers and cache, RAM, disk, archival media","Archival media, disk, RAM, CPU cache"],2,"Registers and cache change fastest, then RAM, then disk, with archival media the most stable. Putting RAM before registers is wrong because registers and cache change even faster than memory.","Domain 3: Order of volatility",["Disk is less volatile than RAM and cache, so it cannot come first.","Registers change faster than RAM, so they must come before it, not after disk.",null,"This lists the order reversed, from least to most volatile."],2],["x48",0,3,"After mapping existing SIEM detections to MITRE ATT&CK, a team sees no coverage for Lateral Movement techniques. What is the best use of this finding?",["Remove the Lateral Movement tactic from the matrix","Report that the network cannot be moved through laterally","Buy a second SIEM to double the existing coverage","Prioritize building detections for relevant Lateral Movement techniques"],3,"ATT&CK coverage mapping is used to find gaps and prioritize new detections based on the threats that matter. A second SIEM running the same rules would still have the same gap.","Domain 3: MITRE ATT&CK",["The matrix describes real adversary behavior; removing a tactic hides the gap rather than fixing it.","No detection coverage means lateral movement would go unseen, not that it is impossible.","Another SIEM with the same rules has the same gap; new detections are needed.",null],2],["x49",0,3,"Memory analysis shows explorer.exe holds a region marked executable and writable that contains a PE header but maps to no file on disk. What does this suggest?",["Code injection into a legitimate process","Normal paging of Windows system memory","A corrupted page file","A pending Windows update staged in memory before the next reboot"],0,"Executable memory with a PE header and no backing file is a classic sign of injected code such as reflective DLL loading. Normal paging does not create writable, executable regions with an unbacked PE image.","Domain 3: Memory analysis",[null,"Paging moves memory to disk; it does not create writable, executable regions with an unbacked PE image.","A corrupt page file would not create an injected PE image inside a running process.","Updates are installed by update services from files on disk, not injected into explorer.exe."],3],["x50",0,3,"During a phishing incident, three users entered credentials into a fake login page. Their passwords have been reset. What additional containment step is most important?",["Delete the phishing email from those three mailboxes only","Revoke their active sessions and tokens and check for new mailbox rules","Reimage the three users' laptops","Disable the three accounts permanently"],1,"Attackers can keep access through existing sessions, tokens and forwarding rules even after a password change. Reimaging the laptops does not help because the credentials were stolen through a web page, not malware on the device.","Domain 3: Containment strategies",["Deleting the email from three mailboxes misses other recipients and does not remove the attacker's access.",null,"The credentials were stolen through a web page; reimaging laptops does not revoke attacker sessions.","Permanently disabling the accounts is excessive once sessions are revoked and passwords reset."],3],["x51",0,4,"An extortion group has stolen data and is threatening to leak it unless paid. Legal counsel approves reporting the crime to outside authorities. Who should be contacted?",["The company's internet service provider","The affected software vendor","Law enforcement","The customers' banks"],2,"Extortion and data theft are crimes, and legal counsel typically coordinates reporting to law enforcement. The ISP can help with traffic issues but has no role in investigating extortion.","Domain 4: Incident response communication",["An ISP handles network connectivity and has no authority to investigate extortion.","The software vendor may help with a flaw but does not investigate crimes.",null,"Customers' banks may later be informed but are not the authority for reporting a crime."],1],["x52",0,4,"Why should an incident response plan define explicit criteria for declaring an incident and assigning severity levels?",["So the SOC can avoid reporting incidents to leadership","So every alert is automatically treated as a breach","So vulnerability scans can be run more often","So analysts escalate consistently and the right people are engaged in time"],3,"Clear declaration and severity criteria make escalation consistent and bring in the right responders and stakeholders. Treating every alert as a breach would overwhelm the team and defeat the purpose of severity levels.","Domain 4: Incident declaration and escalation",["Declaration criteria improve reporting to leadership; they are not a way to avoid it.","Treating every alert as a breach would overwhelm responders and make severity levels pointless.","Incident declaration criteria have nothing to do with scan frequency.",null],1],["x53",0,4,"A vulnerability report shows 12,000 open findings. Executives want to know where to focus. Which addition is most useful?",["A top 10 list of the highest-risk issues ranked by business impact","The full raw scanner export for all 12,000 findings attached as a spreadsheet","Every CVE ID sorted alphabetically","Screenshots of the scanner's configuration pages"],0,"A short, risk-ranked top 10 turns a huge volume of data into clear priorities for decision makers. The raw export contains the data but leaves executives to do the analysis themselves.","Domain 4: Vulnerability reports",[null,"A raw export contains the data but leaves executives to do the analysis themselves.","Alphabetical CVE order says nothing about risk or priority.","Scanner configuration screenshots do not help leadership decide where to focus."],2],["x54",0,4,"Which part of an incident report best shows how long the attacker was present and how quickly each response step happened?",["Executive summary","Timeline of events","Recommendations","Glossary"],1,"The timeline records timestamps for attacker actions and response steps, showing dwell time and response speed. The executive summary gives a brief overview but not a step-by-step chronology.","Domain 4: Incident reports",["The executive summary is a brief overview, not a detailed chronology.",null,"Recommendations describe what to improve, not when each event happened.","A glossary defines terms and contains no event times."],1],["x55",0,4,"A vendor announces a zero-day in the company's VPN appliance, with no patch available yet. What should the analyst's update to leadership include?",["Only the CVSS score, since there is nothing to patch","A recommendation to wait quietly until a patch is released","Exposure, available mitigations or workarounds, and a plan to patch once a fix ships","A request to replace all VPN hardware immediately"],2,"With no patch, leadership needs to know the exposure and what can be done now, plus the plan for the eventual fix. A score alone gives no guidance on action, and waiting silently leaves the risk unmanaged.","Domain 4: Zero-day reporting",["A score alone gives leadership no guidance on what to do before a patch exists.","Staying quiet leaves the exposure unmanaged and leadership uninformed.",null,"Replacing all hardware is a drastic cost that may not be needed if workarounds exist."],3],["x56",0,4,"After detection tuning, alert volume fell 60% while the number of confirmed incidents detected stayed the same. How should this be reported?",["As a detection failure, because fewer alerts means weaker security","As evidence that attacks against the company have declined","As a reason to remove the tuned rules completely","As improved efficiency: less noise with no loss of detection"],3,"Fewer alerts with the same confirmed detections means analysts spend less time on noise without missing real incidents. It says nothing about attack volume, because the number of incidents found did not change.","Domain 4: Response metrics",["Detection held steady; only noise fell, so this is not a failure.","Confirmed incidents stayed the same, so there is no evidence that attacks declined.","Removing rules that cut noise without losing detections would undo a real improvement.",null],3],["x57",0,4,"A critical vulnerability affects a line-of-business application. Who should the analyst work with to plan and schedule remediation?",["The system or application owner","The public relations and corporate communications team","External auditors","Law enforcement"],0,"The system owner understands business impact and can approve maintenance windows and changes. Auditors review compliance afterward but do not own or schedule fixes.","Domain 4: Stakeholder communication",[null,"PR handles public messaging, not technical remediation schedules.","Auditors assess compliance afterward and do not own or schedule fixes.","Law enforcement investigates crimes and has no role in routine patching."],1],["x58",0,4,"Which metric best shows whether previously fixed vulnerabilities keep coming back?",["Total number of scans run","Recurrence rate of closed findings","Number of assets discovered","Average CVSS score of new findings"],1,"Recurrence rate tracks how often closed findings reappear, pointing to problems such as outdated images or configuration drift. Scores of new findings measure severity, not repeats.","Domain 4: Metrics and KPIs",["The number of scans shows activity, not whether fixed issues return.",null,"Asset counts show inventory size, not repeat findings.","Average severity of new findings says nothing about old findings coming back."],1],["x59",0,4,"A draft incident report states that the attacker likely got in through phishing, but it cites no email or log evidence. What is the best improvement?",["Delete the root cause section entirely","State phishing as a certain fact to sound authoritative","Cite supporting evidence, or state the confidence level and evidence gaps","Move the statement into the executive summary"],2,"Report conclusions should be tied to evidence, and where evidence is missing the confidence and gaps should be stated. Presenting an unproven guess as fact can mislead remediation and legal decisions.","Domain 4: Incident reports",["Removing the root cause removes the most useful part of the report instead of supporting it.","Stating an unproven guess as fact can mislead remediation and legal decisions.",null,"Moving an unsupported claim into the summary gives it more prominence without fixing the lack of evidence."],3],["x60",0,4,"An audit finds 15 controls not met. Which document lists each gap with its planned fix, owner and target dates?",["Chain of custody form for audit evidence","Memorandum of understanding","Rules of engagement","Plan of action and milestones"],3,"A plan of action and milestones is the action plan tracking each weakness, remediation, owner and deadline. An MOU sets out an agreement between parties, not a remediation schedule.","Domain 4: Compliance reports and action plans",["A chain of custody form tracks evidence handling, not control gaps.","An MOU sets out an agreement between parties, not a remediation schedule.","Rules of engagement define the scope and limits of a test, not a remediation plan.",null],2]]);
